    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. I did notice air leakage at the wall between the orginal house and the addition, and through the ductwork that runs through the crawlspace. I also noted air leakage through your attic hatch, through the ductwork into the closet under the stairs in the addition, and around the newly installed propane heaters in the addition. 



I checked for air leakage at both your bathroom fans. While I did not find significant air leakage at these fans, I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $175&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of the original half of your house appeat to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x3 strapping with R8 insulation. The walls of your addition are much thicker, and I have assumed 2x8 walls with R24 insulation, with additional 2x4 framing and R12 insulation on the outside. I have modelled replacing the 2x3 strapping on the main part of the house with 4.5 inches of rigid insulation, which represents approximately 44% of the total wall area of your house. I have not modelled an increase to the walls of the addition, as these walls are already well insulated and any savings would be minimal.  



This upgrade is the most cost effective if done at the same time as a siding replacement. Additional savings will be achieved insulating your floor headers at the same time. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing your wall insulation as described above, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$450 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawlspace appears to be 2x10 joists with R32 insulation. I have modelled adding an additional three inches of rigid insulation below the floor joists for the entire crawlspace. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid insulation board below the joists of the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. You must add a minimum of R24 to the floor to be eligible for any rebates under the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

Most of your windows are newer, triple paned windows with a Low E coating. However, I did find that the two windows on either side of your front door are still double pane windows that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading these two windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. 



In addition, should you need to replace any of your other windows for any reason, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. I did notice some fogging on the picture window above the stairwell that may indicate that the seals are gone, in which case you should consider replacing this window. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your two front windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $25&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <doors>

As your front door appears to be a wooden door, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR® qualified door rated for climate zone 3. I also recommend that you check the condition of the weather stripping around all of your doors, to reduce air leakage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your front door with ENERGY STAR® qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <AdditionalComments>

HEATING



Your current propane fired furnace was originally rated at 96% AFUE. As with your instantaneous hot water tank, I do not recommend replacing your furnace at this time. When it is time to replace it, I recommend an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



I have modelled the impact of using your two propane wall heaters to meet 50% of the heating load in your addition. Because your wall heaters burn propane less efficiently than your main furnace, using them will increase your annual heating costs by approximately $260. I recommend that you take this into account when using your wall heaters. 



During the blower door test, I also noted a significant amount of air leakage coming up through the ducts that run through the crawlspace. Although it appears that the ducts are insulated, I strongly recommend making sure that the ducts are sealed properly, and reinsulating as necessary. 



DOMESTIC HOT WATER



You already have a propane fired, instantaneous hot water tank with an energy factor of 0.9, and I do not recommend replacing this at this time. When this appliance is at the end of it's service life, I recommend replacing it with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



You also have a small electric hot water tank installed in the upstair bathroom. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I have modelled adding an R5 insulation blanket to this small electrical tank to reduce standby losses. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an R5 insulation blanket to the electric hot water tank in your upstairs bathroom, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for this from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;





ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;





ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

